<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freedom Charter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Freedom Charter is the statement of principles adopted by the Congress of the People at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kliptown">Kliptown</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> on 26 June 1955, organised by the ANC and its allies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-alliance">Congress Alliance</a>. Opening with the words '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> belongs to all who live in it, black and white', it called for equal rights, a non-racial democracy and a fair share of the country's wealth. It led to the Treason Trial and became the core programme of the ANC.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/freedom-charter.jpg" alt="Freedom Charter" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joonasl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kliptown_Freedom_Charter_Memorial_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Charter grew out of about a decade of resistance to white minority rule and followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/defiance-campaign">Defiance Campaign</a> of 1952, at a time when the government was moving to stamp out dissent outside parliament. In 1955 the ANC sent 50,000 volunteers into the townships and the countryside to collect freedom demands from the people, such as land for the landless, living wages and shorter hours, and free compulsory education regardless of colour. ANC leaders including Z. K. Mathews, Lionel Bernstein, Ethel Drus, Ruth First and Alan Lipman drew these into one document, which Beata Lipman wrote out by hand.</p><p>About 3,000 people gathered for the Congress of the People. Police broke up the meeting on its second day, but by then the whole Charter had been read and each section approved with cries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>! and Mayibuye! <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, restricted by banning orders, escaped the police disguised as a milkman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Its sections each open with a demand: The People Shall Govern!, equal rights for all national groups, a share for the people in the country's wealth, land shared among those who work it, equality before the law, equal human rights, work and security, open doors of learning and culture, houses, security and comfort, and peace and friendship. The Charter called for votes for every man and woman, the repeal of apartheid and pass laws, the transfer of mineral wealth, banks and monopoly industry to the people, a forty-hour week and minimum wage, free and compulsory education, and the right of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> and of all African peoples to decide their own future.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Charter broke with earlier civil rights politics by calling for a fundamental restructuring of South African society, and its commitment to a non-racial country has remained the ANC's platform. Members with Africanist views left over it and formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">Pan Africanist Congress</a>. The government denounced the Congress as treason and arrested 156 activists, Mandela among them, who were all acquitted in the Treason Trial. The Charter circulated underground and inspired young militants in the 1980s, and the Constitution adopted after the democratic elections of 1994 took up many of its demands. The original is kept at Liliesleaf Farm, now a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Freedom Charter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in 1923, was founded at Bloemfontein in 1912; Clements Kadalie’s Industrial and Commercial Workers’ Union organised tens of thousands of workers in the 1920s; and in the 1950s the ANC and its allies ran the Defiance Campaign of 1952 and adopted the <b>Freedom Charter</b> at Kliptown in 1955.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The ANC’s Programme of Action of 1949 called for boycotts, strikes and civil disobedience, and in 1955 the Congress Alliance, which joined the ANC with Indian, coloured and white congresses and a trade union federation, adopted the <b>Freedom Charter</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The ANC had been banned, and although the <b>Freedom Charter</b> stayed in circulation, for many students the ANC had disappeared.</blockquote><a href="/movement/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
